window.__registerChapter({"num": 38, "slug": "038_Genetic_Etiologies_of_Hemolytic_Uremic_Syndrome", "title": "Genetic Etiologies of Hemolytic Uremic Syndrome", "authors": "Marina Noris, Miriam Rigoldi, Giliane Nanchen, Giuseppe Remuzzi", "part_num": "VI", "part_title": "Renal Disorders", "word_count": 8711, "pearls": ["Hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS) belongs to a group of disorders collectively known as thrombotic microangiopathies (TMA) and is described as an acute syndrome defined by a clinical triad of thrombocytopenia, nonimmune microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, and acute kidney failure.", "TTP is caused by a deficiency of the von Willebrand factor (VWF)-cleaving protease ADAMTS13, which can be either congenital or acquired.", "Genetic abnormalities are the most common causes of aHUS.", "STEC-HUS is by far the most common form, responsible for 85–90% of pediatric HUS; the epidemiology of HUS reflects that of Stx- E. coli hemorrhagic colitis.", "In Tuscany (Italy), the expanded newborn screening of approximately 600,000 neonates revealed a birth prevalence of cblC-type disorder of ~1:26,000.", "MMA-cblC type has an autosomal recessive digenic inheritance, associated with pathogenic variants in MMACHC and/or the neighboring PRDX1 , the latter resulting in MMACHC silencing.", "The lack of the protein encoded by MMACHC causes an intracellular deficiency of the metabolically active forms of cobalamin (methylcobalamin and adenosylcobalamin), which are essential cofactors for remethylating homocysteine to methionine and converting methylmalonic acid to succinic acid.", "The early-onset form is characterized by lethargy, failure to thrive, developmental delay, neurological symptoms, microcephaly, ocular abnormalities and pulmonary arterial hypertension."], "genes": ["ADAMTS13", "C3", "C5", "CD46", "CFB", "CFH", "CFHR1", "CFHR3", "CFHR4", "CFI", "DGKE", "G6PD", "INF2", "IVS12", "MCP2", "MMACHC", "PRDX1", "THBD"], "omims": [], "subtopics": ["Epidemiology", "Clinical Presentation", "Differential Diagnosis and Laboratory Investigations", "Laboratory Evaluation for Suspected HUS", "Histology", "Genetics of aHUS", "Complement-Mediated aHUS (C-aHUS)", "Complement Factor H", "Membrane Cofactor Protein (MCP)", "Complement Factor I (CFI)", "Thrombomodulin", "Complement Factor B and C3", "Determinants of Disease Penetrance", "Genotype-Phenotype Correlations"], "figs": [{"img": "fig_p0847_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 38.1", "caption": "Differential diagnostic algorithm in thrombotic microangiopathies. Ab, Antibodies; aHUS,"}, {"img": "vfig_p0846_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 38.2", "caption": "Peripheral blood smear stained with hematoxylin and eosin (100× magnification) demonstrating fragmentation of red blood cells (schistocytes) associated with the thrombotic microangiopathy syndromes."}, {"img": "fig_p0848_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 38.2", "caption": "Peripheral blood smear stained with hematoxylin and eosin (100× magnification) demonstrating fragmentation of red blood cells (schistocytes) associated with the thrombotic microangiopathy syndromes."}, {"img": "fig_p0849_14.jpg", "label": "Fig. 38.3", "caption": "Kidney biopsies of patients with aHUS. (A) A 2-year-old female patient carrying a pathogenic variant in DGKE , who presented with acute kidney injury, hemolysis, normal platelet count, and nephrotic syndrome. Biopsy demonstrates both arteriolar (*) and capillary (arrow) thrombi as well as diffuse glomerular mesangiolysis and basal membrane duplication. Stain: Acid Fuchsin Orange G. (Courtesy from Dr. H. Chehade and Dr. S. Rotman, Lausanne University Hospital, Switzerland.) (B) A 36-year-old male patient with recurrent aHUS associated to a heterozygous pathogenic variant in the complement factor H gene. Biopsy demonstrates extensive capillary thrombosis with an aspect of glomerular paralysis, with capillary loops containing abundant erythrocytes, karyorrhexis with cellular debris in capillary lumina, and severe mesangiolysis. Stain: periodic acid–Schiff stain."}, {"img": "fig_p0849_15.jpg", "label": "Fig. 38.3", "caption": "Kidney biopsies of patients with aHUS. (A) A 2-year-old female patient carrying a pathogenic variant in DGKE , who presented with acute kidney injury, hemolysis, normal platelet count, and nephrotic syndrome. Biopsy demonstrates both arteriolar (*) and capillary (arrow) thrombi as well as diffuse glomerular mesangiolysis and basal membrane duplication. Stain: Acid Fuchsin Orange G. (Courtesy from Dr. H. Chehade and Dr. S. Rotman, Lausanne University Hospital, Switzerland.) (B) A 36-year-old male patient with recurrent aHUS associated to a heterozygous pathogenic variant in the complement factor H gene. Biopsy demonstrates extensive capillary thrombosis with an aspect of glomerular paralysis, with capillary loops containing abundant erythrocytes, karyorrhexis with cellular debris in capillary lumina, and severe mesangiolysis. Stain: periodic acid–Schiff stain."}, {"img": "fig_p0850_06.jpg", "label": "Fig. 38.4", "caption": "The complement cascade. Schematic overview of the complement cascade illustrating the three activation pathways (classical, lectin and alternative) and the membrane attack complex. The alternative pathway (AP) is continuously activated in plasma by low-grade hydrolysis of C3. The latter binds factor B, to form a C3(H2O)B complex. Factor D (CFD) cleaves factor B to form the AP initiation C3 convertase that cleaves C3 to C3b. The activation is then amplified by the covalent binding of a small amount of C3b to hydroxyl groups on cell-surface carbohydrates and proteins of target cells such as bacterial cells. This C3b binds factor B, to form the amplification loop C3 convertase C3bBb. C3 convertase enzymes cleave many molecules of C3, resulting in a positive feedback amplification loop. C3b also binds to the C3 convertase forming the C5 convertase enzyme C3b2Bb. The AP is highly regulated as to prevent nonspecific damage to host cells and limit the deposition of complement to the surface of pathogens. This fine regulation occurs through a number of membrane-anchored and fluid phase regulators. CFH, Complement factor H (acts as cofactor for factor I for C3b cleavage and favors the decay of the C3 convertase of the AP); CFI, complement factor I (degrades C3b and C4b); MCP, membrane cofactor protein (binds C3b and C4b and has cofactor activity); THBD, thrombomodulin (favors endothelial cellsurface C3b inactivation)."}, {"img": "vfig_p0849_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 38.4", "caption": "The complement cascade. Schematic overview of the complement cascade illustrating the three activation pathways (classical, lectin and alternative) and the membrane attack complex. The alternative pathway (AP) is continuously activated in plasma by low-grade hydrolysis of C3. The latter binds factor B, to form a C3(H2O)B complex. Factor D (CFD) cleaves factor B to form the AP initiation C3 convertase that cleaves C3 to C3b. The activation is then amplified by the covalent binding of a small amount of C3b to hydroxyl groups on cell-surface carbohydrates and proteins of target cells such as bacterial cells. This C3b binds factor B, to form the amplification loop C3 convertase C3bBb. C3 convertase enzymes cleave many molecules of C3, resulting in a positive feedback amplification loop. C3b also binds to the C3 convertase forming the C5 convertase enzyme C3b2Bb. The AP is highly regulated as to prevent nonspecific damage to host cells and limit the deposition of complement to the surface of pathogens. This fine regulation occurs through a number of membrane-anchored and fluid phase regulators. CFH, Complement factor H (acts as cofactor for factor I for C3b cleavage and favors the decay of the C3 convertase of the AP); CFI, complement factor I (degrades C3b and C4b); MCP, membrane cofactor protein (binds C3b and C4b and has cofactor activity); THBD, thrombomodulin (favors endothelial cellsurface C3b inactivation)."}], "tables_meta": [{"label": "Table 38.1", "title": "Score Components Points"}, {"label": "TABLE 38.3", "title": "Gene Ab normalities Associated With aHUS"}, {"label": "TABLE 38.4", "title": "Risk Haplotypes for aHUS"}, {"label": "TABLE 38.5", "title": "Eculiz Uremi umab Dose for T c Syndrome reatment of Hemolytic"}, {"label": "Table 38.5", "title": "Body Weight (kg) Induction Maint enance"}], "body_md": "\n<!-- p.844 -->\n\n# 38 Genetic Etiologies of Hemolytic Uremic Syndrome \n\n**MARINA NORIS, MIRIAM RIGOLDI, GILIANE NANCHEN, GIUSEPPE REMUZZI** \n\nHemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS) belongs to a group of disorders collectively known as **thrombotic microangiopathies (TMA)** and is described as an acute syndrome defined by a clinical triad of thrombocytopenia, nonimmune microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, and acute kidney failure. \n\nThe umbrella term TMA describes a histopathological process of endothelial injury and ensuing thrombotic vascular lesions, which regroups various diseases, such as HUS, thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP), or the hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelets (HELLP) syndrome. \n\nDespite their diverse etiologies, all TMAs present with a common clinical picture of consumptive thrombocytopenia, hemolytic anemia and organ dysfunction. Formation of fibrin and platelet-rich thrombi in the microcirculation obstructs vessel lumina, leading to end-organ ischemia and infarction. This translates into thickening of the vascular walls in arterioles and capillaries, prominent endothelial swelling and detachment, as well as subendothelial accumulation of proteins and cell debris. The susceptibility of kidney vasculature to endothelial injury makes acute kidney failure one of the major features of HUS. Nevertheless, other organs such as the brain, heart, intestines and pancreas may also be involved. \n\nThe term HUS has been used to describe forms of TMA with predominant kidney involvement, whereas the term TTP referred to TMA with prevailing central nervous system manifestations. The two entities were distinguished based on different etiological mechanisms, leading to the development of useful tests for the differential diagnosis and paving the way for specific treatments. \n\nTTP is caused by a deficiency of the von Willebrand factor (VWF)-cleaving protease ADAMTS13, which can be either congenital or acquired. The etiological classification of HUS, however, is less well-defined, due to the multiplicity of possible causative mechanisms. \n\nThe most common cause of HUS is infection with **Shiga-like toxin-producing bacteria (Stx)** , particularly enterohemorrhagic _Escherichia coli_ (STEC), but also \n\n_Shigella dysenteria_ . HUS then develops as a complication of a bacterial gastroenteritis. The released Shiga toxins damage the intestinal blood vessels and enter the bloodstream by binding to blood cells, reaching the target organs, particularly the kidney. Shiga toxin is internalized into glomerular endothelial cells and inhibits protein synthesis, causing cell death. It also promotes leukocyte-dependent inflammation and a prothrombotic state in endothelial cells as well as complement hyperactivation, which contributes to microangiopathic lesions in STEC-HUS. Cytotoxicity of Shiga toxin is also evidenced in podocytes and renal tubules. STEC-induced HUS has a typical presentation of prodromal, often bloody, diarrhea, followed within a few days by HUS. \n\nThe remaining cases of HUS, not associated with a Shiga toxin-producing bacteria, are often gathered under the general denomination of **atypical HUS (aHUS)** . This denomination regroups various etiologies, both primary and secondary, the latter implying the presence of another underlying disease. The term aHUS is preferably reserved for primary cases and is sometimes even specifically associated with forms caused by dysregulation of the complement alternative pathway. \n\nGenetic abnormalities are the most common causes of aHUS. These include: \n\n1. Complement-mediated aHUS (C-aHUS) \n\n2. _DGKE_ gene-associated aHUS \n\n3. Cobalamin (Cbl) metabolism-associated aHUS \n\n4. Other rare genetic forms \n\nIt is important to understand that the classification and interpretation of the role of genetic components in aHUS are complicated by the interaction between inherited predisposition and environmental triggers. Some patients classified as having a secondary form of HUS, because of an identified external underlying disease process may have a concomitant genetic predisposition involving the complement system. The latter cases are predominantly due to a genetic predisposition to complement dysregulation in whom a coexisting condition _triggered_ the acute HUS episode.\n\n<!-- p.845 -->\n\n**Pregnancy-associated HUS** typically illustrates this challenge for classification, in that pathogenic variants of the complement genes are found with the same prevalence (40–60%) as in C-aHUS. Pregnancy-associated HUS is not considered a secondary form but rather a primary genetic complement-mediated HUS in which pregnancy acts as the trigger. \n\nApproximately 30% of primary aHUS remains _without_ any identified etiology. \n\n## **Epidemiology** \n\nThe epidemiology varies greatly according to age, subtype, and geographical area. The global _prevalence_ of HUS is estimated ~2–3 per 100,000 with an annual _incidence_ ~0.6 per 100,000. The incidence of HUS is highest in young children; 5 per 100,000 in children under 5 years old. STEC-HUS is by far the most common form, responsible for 85–90% of pediatric HUS; the epidemiology of HUS reflects that of Stx- _E. coli_ hemorrhagic colitis. \n\nThe incidence of Stx- _E. coli_ hemorrhagic colitis is highest in the summer months and varies by geographical area. It is common in South America, where the incidence rises up to 10–17 cases per 100,000 children under 5 years old in Argentina. \n\nAfter exposure to Stx- _E. coli_ , ~60% of individuals develop hemorrhagic colitis. STEC-induced hemorrhagic colitis in adults is usually a self-limiting condition, progressing to HUS only in a minority of cases (5% of adult cases in sporadic infections and up to 20% of cases during epidemics). Children <10 years have a higher risk (15–20%) of developing HUS complications because anti-STEC antibodies may develop later in life. STEC-HUS constitutes one of the most frequent causes of acute renal injury in children. \n\nOther infections such as invasive infection with _Streptococcus pneumoniae_ may rarely be complicated by aHUS. Children <2 years of age have the highest risk; this form accounts for 5–20% of reported non STEC-associated pediatric cases, while it remains rare in adults. Genetic forms represent about 5–10% of all cases of HUS. \n\nThe prevalence of _C-aHUS_ across all age groups is ~5 per million; the range is 2.2 to 9.4 per million population before 20 years of age. The annual incidence for all ages combined is ~0.23 to 2 per million, whereas the incidence for younger patients, up to 20 years of age is ~0.26 and 0.75 per million. Genetic or acquired complement dysregulation is present in 60% of cases. \n\nInformation about the epidemiology of **_DGKE_ -associated aHUS** varies, perhaps by region. In a French report, ~30% of genetic aHUS occurring during the first year of age were linked to homozygous or compound heterozygous pathogenic variants in _DGKE_ . In a Spanish cohort, among 83 aHUS children with onset before 2 years of age, only 5% had _DGKE_ pathogenic variants. Among 64 patients with early-onset primary aHUS (before 5 years), identified in an International Registry of HUS/TTP and who were screened \n\nfor _DGKE_ variants, only 5 cases (7%) had biallelic variants in this gene, with an age of onset ranging from 6 months to 5 years. \n\nIn disorders of **cobalamin (cbl)-metabolism** , neonatal screening programs have revealed a notably higher occurrence of the cblC-type than formerly estimated, ranging from 1:37,000 to 1:100,000. In Tuscany (Italy), the expanded newborn screening of approximately 600,000 neonates revealed a birth prevalence of cblC-type disorder of ~1:26,000. CblD-, cblE-, and cblG-types are rarer than cblC. Atypical HUS can occur in all forms. \n\nCases of atypical HUS associated with _Denys-Drash syndrome_ , _primary hyperoxaluria type II_ , _G6PD-deficiency_ , and the syndrome associated with _TREX1_ have been reported anecdotally. \n\n## **Clinical Presentation** \n\nThe clinical signs and symptoms of aHUS are nonspecific and include oliguria/anuria in the context of rapid-onset acute kidney injury. Significant arterial hypertension is often present, making it challenging to understand whether hypertension is the cause or a consequence of TMA. Severe hemolytic anemia might present with pallor, jaundice, asthenia, tachycardia, and exertional dyspnea. Hemorrhage may occur from severe thrombocytopenia. \n\nExtrarenal complications can occur in up to 20% of cases. The central nervous system is the second most affected organ, manifesting with convulsions, altered consciousness (lethargy, confusion, coma), cortical blindness, and hemiparesis. Less frequently, involvement of the cardiovascular system (cardiomyopathy, myocardial infarction, heart failure), lungs (pulmonary hemorrhage), gastrointestinal system (pancreatitis, intestinal bleeding), or musculoskeletal injury (rhabdomyolysis) may develop. A minority of patients display a subacute onset without the complete clinical picture. \n\n## **Differential Diagnosis and Laboratory Investigations** \n\nThe etiology is important for the prognosis, the treatment, and the risk of relapse (Fig. 38.1). In patients with an extrinsic and transient factor, the thrombotic microangiopathy process usually regresses once the underlying cause has been removed or treated. When there is a genetic etiology, relapses are frequent and specific treatment is fundamental to prevent relapses, progression, and improve the long-term prognosis. All cases of HUS present with an acute kidney injury and may rapidly progress to end-stage kidney disease. Blood sampling for collecting plasma and serum is mandatory before starting any treatment that could affect results (Table 38.1). Routine hematological and biochemical assays will rapidly highlight the classical triad of all HUS forms, consisting of: \n\n1. Mechanical, nonimmune, **hemolytic anemia** due to the fragmentation of erythrocytes as a consequence of the\n\n<!-- p.846 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0847_02.jpg)\n\n\n- **Fig. 38.1** Differential diagnostic algorithm in thrombotic microangiopathies. _Ab,_ Antibodies; _aHUS,_ \n\n- atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome; _anti-VEGF,_ antivascular endothelial growth factor; _B. pertussis, Bordetella pertussis_ ; _cbl,_ cobalamin; _CFB,_ complement factor B; _CFH,_ complement factor H; _CFHR,_ complement factor H related; _CFI,_ complement factor I; _CMV,_ cytomegalovirus; _EBV,_ Epstein-Barr virus; _ESRD,_ end-stage renal disease _FH,_ factor H; _G6PD,_ glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase; _H1N1,_ influenza A virus subtype H1N1; _HBV,_ hepatitis B virus; _HCV,_ hepatitis C virus; _HELLP,_ hemolysis elevated liver enzymes and low platelets; _HIV,_ human immunodeficiency viruses; _IFN,_ interferon; _INF2,_ inverted formin-2; _M. pneumoniae, Mycoplasma pneumoniae_ ; _MCP,_ membrane cofactor protein; _P. vivax, Plasmodium vivax_ ; _PCR,_ polymerase chain reaction; _RVCL-S,_ retinal vasculopathy with cerebral leukoencephalopathy and systemic manifestations; _S. pneumoniae, Streptococcus pneumoniae_ ; _SARSCoV2,_ severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2; _SLE,_ systemic lupus erythematosus; _STEC-HUS,_ shiga-toxin Escherichia coli hemolytic uremic syndrome _T. gondii: Toxoplasma gondii_ ; _THBD,_ thrombomodulin; _TTP,_ thrombotic thrombopenic purpura; _V. Zoster, varicella zoster_ . \n\n##### **TABLE 38.1** \n\n#### **Laboratory Evaluation for Suspected HUS** \n\n- Complete blood count \n\n- Hemolysis parameters: LDH, haptoglobin, indirect bilirubinemia, peripheral blood smear, Coombs test \n\n- Coagulation testing (INR) \n\n- Creatinine, BUN \n\n- PCR for Shiga toxin in the feces \n\n- ADAMTS13 enzymatic levels \n\n- Complement activity assay: C3, CH50, sC5b-9, seruminduced C5b-9 deposition on cultured endothelial cells \n\n- Anti-factor H antibodies \n\n- Plasma homocysteine \n\n- Autoimmunity: anti-dsDNA, anti-ENA, anti-β2 GPI, anticardiolipin antibodies, lupus anticoagulant \n\n- Serum protein electrophoresis \n\n- Liver and pancreatic enzymes \n\nhigh levels of shear stress in obstructed vessels (Fig. 38.2). High lactate dehydrogenase (LDH), undetectable haptoglobin levels, indirect hyperbilirubinemia and presence of fragmented erythrocytes, or schistocytes (>2%) in the blood peripheral smear confirm intravascular hemolysis. The Coombs test is negative, except in pneumococcalaHUS. \n\n2. **Thrombocytopenia** (or thrombocytes within normal range, but with a reduction of 30% compared to usual values). Thrombocytopenia is partly due to consumption of platelets in microthrombi, even if labeling studies have also shown platelet removal in the reticuloendothelial system. \n\n3. **Acute kidney injury** is always present, by definition, usually with microhematuria and sometimes proteinuria.\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p0846_01.jpg)\n\n\n<!-- p.847 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0848_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 38.2** Peripheral blood smear stained with hematoxylin and eosin (100× magnification) demonstrating fragmentation of red blood cells (schistocytes) associated with the thrombotic microangiopathy syndromes.  (From Dixon BP, Gruppo RA. Atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome. _Pediatr Clin N Am_ . 2018;65:509-525 [Fig. 5, p. 516].) \n\nIn the absence of a specific diagnostic test, aHUS remains a diagnosis of exclusion based on a thorough clinical history, examination and stepwise laboratory investigations. \n\nWhen there is a suspected case of HUS, the first step is to exclude STEC-HUS; TTP represents the main differential diagnosis in adults (Fig. 38.1). \n\nIn _STEC-HUS_ , the illness typically begins with abdominal cramps, vomiting, and diarrhea, which may become hemorrhagic in 70%. Dietary history is key when suspecting STEC-HUS, and one should look for consumption of potentially contaminated water and food in the days preceding the first symptoms. Family members may also be ill but with less severe symptoms. Forty to 50% of cases require dialysis; kidney recovery usually occurs after 1 to 2 weeks. End-stage kidney disease (ESKD) after the initial presentation is uncommon. Nevertheless, long-term kidney sequelae are common with hypertension and chronic kidney disease (CKD) occurring in 25–40% of patients. Neurological involvement is the most frequently reported extrarenal manifestation. The mortality rate in the acute phase of the disease is approximately 2–5% in STEC-HUS, but is higher when HUS follows _Shigella_ infection. Treatment is mostly supportive. \n\nThe genes coding for Shiga toxin can be identified in the feces through polymerase chain reaction (PCR) using DNA isolated from stool specimens. Although the STEC strain O157 was predominantly isolated in patients with STEC– gastroenteritis until 2010, other serotypes are frequent in certain geographic areas. Serology for anti-STEC antibodies may reflect a precedent exposure and does not always indicate an acute infection. \n\nAnother important infectious agent of HUS is severe _Streptococcus pneumoniae_ sepsis or pneumonia _(SP-HUS)_ . Consumption of complement components in serum during the acute phase of the illness suggests a role for complement dysregulation in this form of HUS and justifies a trial of \n\ncomplement blockade in some cases plus targeted antibiotic therapy. \n\nDiagnosis of _SP-HUS_ relies on the evidence of invasive _S. pneumonia_ infection through the identification of positive bacteriological cultures of body fluids or by antigen or nucleic acid detection assays. In contrast to other forms of HUS, the direct Coombs test is positive in 60 to 90% of patients with SP-HUS. This is explained by sialic acids (N-acetylneuraminic acid) cleavage by _S. pneumoniae_ - produced neuraminidase from the glycoproteins on cell membrane, including erythrocytes. Ensuing abnormal exposure of T-antigen results in reaction with serum antiT-antibodies and _in vitro_ polyagglutination of erythrocytes. Desialylation of cells also triggers complement activation via the alternative pathway. \n\nOther infections include _viral_ (cytomegalovirus [CMV], Epstein-Barr virus, varicella zoster virus, parvovirus, human immunodeficiency virus [HIV], West Nile virus, and coronavirus SARS-CoV-2), _bacterial (Bordetella pertussis, Mycoplasma pneumoniae)_ , or _parasitic (Toxoplasma gondii, Plasmodium vivax)_ agents. \n\nThe other important differential diagnosis is **thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP)** . \n\nTTP classically presents with the pentad of thrombocytopenia, microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, fever with neurological and kidney dysfunction. Central nervous system (CNS) involvement is seen in over 90% of patients. Chronic kidney disease is also a common complication, with up to 25% of patients exhibiting a creatinine clearance of less than 40 mL/min during long-term follow-up. Cardiac involvement is prevalent in TTP, ranging from elevation of biomarkers to heart failure, infarction and sudden cardiac death. \n\nTTP is due to a deficient activity of ADAMTS13. An enzymatic activity below 10% is highly suggestive of TTP. This enzymatic deficiency can be either **congenital** (due to homozygosity or compound heterozygosity for pathogenic variants in _ADAMTS13_ ), or **acquired** , with the development of anti-ADAMTS13 inhibitory antibodies. Plasma infusion represents the mainstay of treatment for congenital TTP; recombinant ADAMTS13 is also available to treat congenital TTP. Acquired TTP requires additional removal of the antibodies through plasmapheresis, sometimes in association with immunosuppression to prevent further formation of autoantibodies. Testing for the enzymatic ADAMTS13 activity must be done before the administration of fresh frozen plasma or the introduction of plasmapheresis because plasma contains ADAMTS13 from the healthy donor and may alter the results of the test. \n\nThe ADAMTS13 test is not 100% specific, in that the ADAMTS13 activity may be reduced in the acute phase of HUS and thus very low levels cannot always exclude HUS. Renal involvement in TTP can be moderate, whereas severe thrombocytopenia and neurological symptoms are typically present. Because access to ADAMTS13 testing is not always readily available, differences in clinical presentation can be helpful. The **PLASMIC Score** (Table 38.2) has been validated in an adult population as a seven-component\n\n<!-- p.848 -->\n\n#### **TABLE The PLASMIC Score—Prediction of 38.2 ADAMTS13 Deficiency** \n\n|Score Components|Points|\n|---|---|\n|Thrombocytes<30×10<sup>9</sup>/L|1|\n|Hemolysis (reticulocytes>2.5%, or undetectable<br>haptoglobin or indirect bilirubin>2 mg/dL)|1|\n|No active cancer|1|\n|No history of solid organ or stem-cell transplant|1|\n|Mean corpuscular volume<90 fL|1|\n|International normalized ratio (INR)<1.5|1|\n|Creatinine<2 mg/dL|1|\n|Interpretation of the_PLASMIC_score: 0–4: low risk for severe<br>deficiency; 5: intermediate risk; 6–7: high risk.|ADAMTS13|\n\n\nenzymes (amylase, lipase) and respectively elevated transaminases, which raise the differential diagnosis of HELLP syndrome during pregnancy (Fig. 38.1). \n\n### **Histology** \n\nAlthough kidney histology is not mandatory for diagnosis of aHUS, it may confirm the presence of lesions related to thrombotic microangiopathy and assess the chronicity of renal damage. Lesions can involve the glomeruli, arterioles, and interlobular arteries and evolve to irreversible cortical necrosis (Fig. 38.3). However, the context of thrombocytopenia, anemia, and uremia makes a renal biopsy a high-risk procedure with hemorrhagic complications; it is reserved for exceptional cases and if there is a delayed renal recovery. In secondary forms, kidney biopsy may contribute to the differential diagnosis and may have therapeutic consequences. \n\nclinical prediction tool to predict pretest probability of ADAMTS13 deficiency; it has also been studied in children. \n\nClinical evaluation, especially in adults, should also include an in-depth medical history to identify secondary diseases such as malignant hypertension with chronicity features (associated with organ damage such as a retinopathy and left ventricular hypertrophy), autoimmune diseases (including complement-amplifying conditions like systemic lupus erythematosus, dermatomyositis or coexisting conditions such as medication (calcineurin inhibitors, oral contraceptives, quinine, sirolimus, cytotoxic drugs, antivascular endothelial growth factor agents, interferon), monoclonal gammopathy, stem-cell and solid organ transplantation. \n\nThe presence of either a positive family history or a relapsing disease suggests a primary aHUS with a genetic etiology (Fig. 38.1). \n\nIn a minority of cases, pancreatic and hepatic involvement may become apparent through elevated pancreatic \n\n### **Genetics of aHUS** \n\nGenetic analysis is recommended after exclusion of TTP and STEC-HUS in all patients without an evident secondary cause. In situations of delayed or incomplete recovery of kidney function, a genetic component may be sought even in the presence of a secondary cause or documented STEC infection, to exclude an underlying genetic predisposition (Fig. 38.1). Genetic screening becomes particularly important in cases of relapsing HUS, asynchronous family history of HUS and posttransplant HUS. In addition, genetic screening should be done before kidney transplantation in all patients who developed ESKD following aHUS, to evaluate donor strategy and prevent relapse, as well as in patients who manifest aHUS during pregnancy and postpartum. \n\nNext-generation sequencing (NGS) offers panel analysis of all genes associated with aHUS and its differential diagnosis, \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0849_14.jpg)\n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0849_15.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 38.3** Kidney biopsies of patients with aHUS. (A) A 2-year-old female patient carrying a pathogenic variant in _DGKE_ , who presented with acute kidney injury, hemolysis, normal platelet count, and nephrotic syndrome. Biopsy demonstrates both arteriolar (*) and capillary (arrow) thrombi as well as diffuse glomerular mesangiolysis and basal membrane duplication. Stain: Acid Fuchsin Orange G. (Courtesy from Dr. H. Chehade and Dr. S. Rotman, Lausanne University Hospital, Switzerland.) (B) A 36-year-old male patient with recurrent aHUS associated to a heterozygous pathogenic variant in the complement factor H gene. Biopsy demonstrates extensive capillary thrombosis with an aspect of glomerular paralysis, with capillary loops containing abundant erythrocytes, karyorrhexis with cellular debris in capillary lumina, and severe mesangiolysis. Stain: periodic acid–Schiff stain.\n\n<!-- p.849 -->\n\nconsiderably reducing the time for identification of the specific genetic defect. Full analysis of aHUS-associated genes and testing for antibodies against complement factor H (FH) is recommended because the nature of the underlying genetic defect influences disease progression, the risk of relapses after kidney transplantation and responses to therapies. Treatment of acute episodes should be started rapidly after clinical diagnosis, without waiting for results of genetic and anti-FH antibody tests. \n\n### **Complement-Mediated aHUS (C-aHUS)** \n\nIn patients with C-aHUS, the history is usually unremarkable for exposure to potentially contaminated water or food. \n\nHowever, in ~30% of cases, patients may present with gastrointestinal symptoms such as abdominal pain, vomiting, and diarrhea, bloody in some cases, making the distinction with STEC-HUS difficult. Unlike STEC-HUS which is related to an infectious event limited in time, a genetic background persists and causes a chronic condition with a less favorable prognosis. \n\nC-aHUS is caused by an underlying dysregulation of the complement alternative pathway (Fig. 38.4) due to pathogenic variants in genes encoding for complement proteins and regulators or the presence of anti-FH autoantibodies. To date, abnormalities in at least ten genes have been associated with C-aHUS (Table 38.3). Pathogenic variants in these genes lead to a predisposition to develop aHUS and \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0850_06.jpg)\n\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p0849_01.jpg)\n\n**Fig. 38.4** The complement cascade. Schematic overview of the complement cascade illustrating the three activation pathways (classical, lectin and alternative) and the membrane attack complex. The alternative pathway (AP) is continuously activated in plasma by low-grade hydrolysis of C3. The latter binds factor B, to form a C3(H2O)B complex. Factor D (CFD) cleaves factor B to form the AP initiation C3 convertase that cleaves C3 to C3b. The activation is then amplified by the covalent binding of a small amount of C3b to hydroxyl groups on cell-surface carbohydrates and proteins of target cells such as bacterial cells. This C3b binds factor B, to form the amplification loop C3 convertase C3bBb. C3 convertase enzymes cleave many molecules of C3, resulting in a positive feedback amplification loop. C3b also binds to the C3 convertase forming the C5 convertase enzyme C3b2Bb. The AP is highly regulated as to prevent nonspecific damage to host cells and limit the deposition of complement to the surface of pathogens. This fine regulation occurs through a number of membrane-anchored and fluid phase regulators. _CFH,_ Complement factor H (acts as cofactor for factor I for C3b cleavage and favors the decay of the C3 convertase of the AP); _CFI,_ complement factor I (degrades C3b and C4b); _MCP,_ membrane cofactor protein (binds C3b and C4b and has cofactor activity); _THBD,_ thrombomodulin (favors endothelial cellsurface C3b inactivation).\n\n<!-- p.850 -->\n\n\n#### **TABLE 38.3** Gene Ab normalities Associated With aHUS\n\n||Gene|Abnormality|Main Effect|Frequency|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|Complement-<br>related genes|_CFH_|Heterozygous and (rarely)<br>homozygous variants<br>clustering (50%) in the<br>last 2 exons|Impaired cell-surface complement<br>regulation|20–30%|\n||_CFH/CFHR1-5_|Nonallelic homologous<br>recombinations|Impaired cell-surface complement<br>regulation|5–7%|\n||_CFHR1/CFHR3_<br>_CFHR1/CFHR4_|Homozygous deletion and<br>formation of anti-FH<br>antibodies|Impaired cell-surface complement<br>regulation|5–10%|\n||_MCP_|Heterozygous and (rarely)<br>homozygous variants|Reduced cell surface expression,<br>reduced cofactor activity|8–10%|\n||_CFI_|Heterozygous variants|Reduced secretion, low cofactor<br>activity|4–8%|\n||_C3_|Heterozygous variants|Decreased C3b inactivation|6–9%|\n||_CFB_|Heterozygous variants|C3 convertase stabilization|1–3%|\n||_THBD_|Heterozygous variants|Reduced cell-surface C3b<br>inactivation|3–4%|\n||_Combined_<br>_variants_|Heterozygous variants in<br>2 or 3 complement genes|Impaired cell-surface complement<br>regulation|3–6%|\n||**Gene**|**Abnormality**|**Main Effect**|**Frequency**|\n|Complement-<br>unrelated<br>genes|_DGKE_|Homozygous or compound<br>heterozygous variants|Upregulation of prothrombotic<br>factors, decreased VEGF<br>signaling and apoptosis|Infantile cases|\n||_MMACHC PRDX1_<br>(rarely)|Biallelic variants|Errors of cobalamin intracellular<br>metabolism|10–30% of cblC-type<br>def. cases|\n||_WT-1_|Heterozygous variants|Podocyte decreased secretion of<br>VEGF|Rare|\n||_INF-2_|Heterozygous variants|Endothelial injury or procoagulant<br>state|Rare|\n||_GRHPR_|Biallelic variants|Not yet studied|Rare|\n||G6PD|X-linked|Hemolysis, heme mediated injury|Rare|\n||_TREX1_|Heterozygous|Not yet studied|Rare|\n\n\n_aHUS,_ Atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome; _CFH,_ complement factor H gene; _CFHR,_ complement factor H-related genes; _CFI,_ complement factor I; _DGKE,_ diacylglycerol kinase ε; _FH,_ complement factor H protein; _G6PD,_ glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase; _GRHPR,_ glyoxylate/hydroxypyruvate reductase; _INF-2,_ inverted formin 2; _MCP,_ membrane cofactor protein; _THBD,_ thrombomodulin; _TREX1,_ three prime repair exonuclease 1; _WT-1,_ Wilm tumor. \n\nhave an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern, with incomplete penetrance. Many patients with sporadic cases of C-aHUS inherited their genetic defect from an unaffected parent. It is the _copresence_ of genetic and environmental factors that conjointly result in the occurrence or the absence of the disease. \n\n### **Complement Factor H** \n\nComplement factor H is a plasma glycoprotein that regulates the alternative pathway, both in the fluid phase and on the cell surface, by acting as a cofactor for complement \n\nfactor I (FI) and enhancing dissociation of C3 convertase (Fig. 38.4). In the N-terminus part, there is the complement regulatory domain that interacts with C3b. The two C-terminus domains are important for FH interactions with glycosaminoglycans and C3b bound to cell surface. \n\nOver 120 _CFH_ pathogenic or likely pathogenic variants (Database of Complement Gene Variants; www.complementdb.org), have been identified in ~30% of C-aHUS patients (Table 38.3). These genetic abnormalities are mostly heterozygous and cluster in the cell surface recognition domain. As a consequence, the variant FH maintains the capability to regulate complement in fluid phase, but is not capable\n\n<!-- p.851 -->\n\nof binding cells and thus cannot regulate complement activation on cells, particularly endothelial cells and platelets. This results in the activation of the alternative pathway cascade and the formation of the terminal membrane attack complex (Fig. 38.4). \n\nHowever, only a fraction of _CFH_ variants identified in aHUS affect the N-terminal regulatory domain or other domains. Two studies reported comprehensive functional characterizations of ~100 _CFH_ disease-associated variants. Several assays on variant proteins isolated from patients revealed that ~20% of aHUS-associated _CFH_ variants were not expressed at the protein level or affected the fluid phase and surface complement regulatory activities. These studies also identified important limitations in applying prediction algorithms because a substantial fraction of _CFH_ variants predicted to be pathogenic _in silico_ , were found to be normal in functional assays. Conversely, a few variants predicted to be benign _in silico_ were pathogenic experimentally. \n\nComplex genomic abnormalities in _CFH_ have also been reported in patients with aHUS. These events are favored by the presence in the _CFH_ locus of 5 genes _(CFHR1, CFHR2, CFHR3, CFHR4, CFHR5)_ that are homologous to _CFH_ gene, but encode for five factor H-related proteins (FHR1, FHR2, FHR3, FHR4, FHR5) without complement regulatory properties. Such homologies favor nonallelic homologous recombinations that give rise to genomic structural variants. Hybrid _CFH/CFHR1_ and _CFH/CFHR3_ genes encoding for abnormal FH proteins occur in ~5% of patients with aHUS. In these situations, the carboxy-terminal domains, that mediate complement regulation on cell surface, are substituted by those of FHR1 or by the entire FHR3 (Table 38.3). In all cases, the hybrid molecules lose the ability to protect endothelial cells from complement activation. \n\nOther genomic rearrangements have been described that result in a reverse hybrid protein that contains the first four repeats of FHR1 and the C-terminal portion of FH. The fusion protein contains the surface recognition domain of FH, but lacks the complement regulatory activity, so that it acts as a competitor of FH for C3b and its surface ligands. \n\n**Anti-FH inhibitory antibodies** have been reported in 5 to 10% of all patients and ~25 to 50% of pediatric cases. These autoantibodies predominantly target the C-terminal end of FH, impairing complement regulation on host cell surfaces. The development of anti-FH autoantibodies in C-aHUS has a genetic predisposition, being strongly associated with the homozygous deletion of _CFHR1_ and _CFHR3_ (Table 38.3). The combination of the homozygous _CFHR1_ deletion and age at disease onset between 4 and 12 years is highly suggestive of anti-FH antibodies-associated aHUS. Rare complement gene variants identified in 25% of patients with anti-FH autoantibodies may impact disease severity and outcome. \n\n### **Membrane Cofactor Protein (MCP)** \n\nMembrane cofactor protein is a transmembrane complement regulator widely expressed on the surface of endothelial cells. \n\nMCP acts as a cofactor for FI to cleave C3b and C4b (Fig. 38.4). _MCP/CD46_ gene abnormalities account for 8 to 10% C-aHUS cases. Most are heterozygous and cause either reduced protein expression or decreased cofactor activity (Table 38.3). \n\n### **Complement Factor I (CFI)** \n\nComplement factor I is a plasma serine protease that regulates the three complement pathways by cleaving C3b and C4b in the presence of cofactors (Fig. 38.4). _CFI_ heterozygous pathogenic variants have been identified in 4 to 8% of patients (Table 38.3). All these variants are heterozygous and result either in reduced protein levels or impaired C3b proteolytic activity. \n\n### **Thrombomodulin** \n\nHeterozygous variants in _THBD_ encoding thrombomodulin, an endothelial surface anticoagulant protein have been reported in 3 to 4% of patients with C-aHUS(57). Thrombomodulin regulates complement by favoring FH cofactor activity and by promoting the enzymatic activity of TAFI that degrades the C3a and C5a anaphylatoxins (Fig. 38.4). _THBD_ pathogenic variants are associated with reduced C3b degradation and impaired TAFI activity (Table 38.3). \n\n### **Complement Factor B and C3** \n\nGain-of-function heterozygous variants in the _CFB_ and _C3_ genes encoding the alternative pathway C3 convertase components, complement factor B (FB) and C3 (Fig. 38.4), have been reported in 9% and 2% of C-aHUS cases, respectively (Table 38.3). Variant C3 proteins have a defect in binding to complement regulators FH and/or MCP, or CR1, that resulted in a decrease in C3b proteolytic inactivation or an increased affinity for FB, and formed a hyperactive C3 convertase. \n\n_CFB_ gain-of-function variants result in enhanced C3 convertase formation or in a convertase resistant to decay by FH. However, functional studies revealed that about 60% of FB variants have a normal activity and are most likely unrelated to C-aHUS pathogenesis. \n\nThe consequence of pathogenic variants in _C3_ and _CFB_ is hyperactivation of the alternative pathway both in solid and in fluid phase as reflected by severely reduced C3 levels in patients with these variants. \n\n### **Determinants of Disease Penetrance** \n\nComplement-mediated aHUS is not a classic monogenic disorder. Indeed, penetrance in carriers of complement gene abnormalities is largely incomplete and ranges from 20 to 50%. \n\nA second or a third pathogenic variant in complement genes occurs in approximately 6% of C-aHUS patients.\n\n<!-- p.852 -->\n\n\n#### **TABLE 38.4** Risk Haplotypes for aHUS\n\n|Gene|Variant (SNP, rs)|Effect|Haplotype|Impact|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|_CFH_|−332T (c.−332C>T, rs3753394)|Lower FH levels|||\n||2016G (c.2016A>G, p.Q672Q, rs3753396)||||\n||2808T (c.2808G>T, p.E936D, rs1065489)||||\n||||H3 (TGT)|Risk|\n|_CFHR1_|469T (c.469C>T, p.H157Y, rs425757)|Competition with FH at<br>|||\n||475G (c.475C>G, p.L159V, rs410232)|cell surface level|||\n||523C (c.523G>C, p.E175Q, rs388862)||||\n||||CFHR1*B (TGC)|Risk|\n|_MCP/CD46_|−652A (c.−652A>G, rs2796267)|Lower transcriptional<br>|||\n||−366A (c.−366A>G, rs2796268)|activity|||\n||IVS9-78G>A (rs1962149)||||\n||IVS12+638G>A (rs859705)||||\n||c.*783T (c.*783T>C, rs7144)||||\n||||_MCP2 (GGAAC)_|Risk|\n\n\n_aHUS,_ Atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome; _CFH,_ complement factor H; _CFHR1,_ complement factor H-related 1; _MCP,_ membrane cofactor protein; _SNP,_ single nucleotide polymorphism. \n\nCommon genetic risk variants (single-nucleotide polymorphisms [SNPs] and haplotype blocks) in _CFH_ , _MCP_ , and _CFHR1_ act as susceptibility factors for the development of aHUS (Table 38.4) and are synergistic with rare pathogenic variants in determining penetrance. A study in 103 C-aHUS pedigrees showed that both the _CFH_ and _MCP_ risk haplotypes, in patients with pathogenic variants in one or two complement genes, increased the disease penetrance twofold to threefold. Sequencing of the whole panel of disease-associated genes is recommended in C-aHUS, because of increasing evidence that one single patient may have more than one gene abnormality. \n\nEven in subjects with multiple genetic susceptibility factors, C-aHUS may not occur until a precipitating event, such as a respiratory or gastrointestinal infection, pregnancy, or transplantation, triggers endothelial injury and activates complement, unmasking genetically determined complement regulatory defects. \n\n### **Genotype-Phenotype Correlations** \n\nIn C-aHUS, identification of the specific complement genetic defects has prognostic value for disease onset, patient outcome and disease recurrence after kidney transplantation. Complement-related aHUS mostly affects children, but it may manifest at any age. The earliest onset (0 to 5 years) is reported in patients with _CFH_ abnormalities. Most patients with anti-FH antibodies develop the disease in childhood. The combination of the homozygous _CFHR1_ deletion and \n\nage at disease onset between 4 and 12 years is associated the presence of anti-FH antibodies. \n\nOne study in genotype-matched cohorts reported that for patients with _CFH_ , _CFI_ and _C3_ gene abnormalities, the 5-year ESKD-free survival is significantly better in the cohort treated with the anti-C5 antibody eculizumab than in the historical untreated control cohort; renal survival is good in treated and untreated patients with _MCP_ pathogenic variants. \n\nThe risk of _recurrence_ in the kidney graft is influenced by the genetic background. Patients with pathogenic variants in _CFH_ or _CFI_ or _C3_ or _CFB_ , which are all circulating proteins mainly synthesized by the liver, are at high risk of recurrence after transplant because the variant proteins circulate and will reiterate the disease in the graft. Transplant outcome is usually good in patients with isolated _MCP/ CD46_ pathogenic variants. MCP is not a circulating but a surface protein highly expressed in the kidney and the transplant corrects the genetic defect. The subgroup of patients with _MCP/CD46_ variants who experienced recurrence in the graft carried additional genetic abnormalities affecting circulating proteins. In this context, results of complement gene screening have been proposed as a tool for pretransplant stratification of C-aHUS patients to enable individualized complement blockade prophylaxis based on the risk of recurrence. \n\nSimilarly, retrospective and prospective studies show that the presence or absence of a genetic abnormality is the most reliable predictor of relapse of C-aHUS after eculizumab\n\n<!-- p.853 -->\n\ndiscontinuation. Thirty to 60% of patients with complement gene pathogenic variants relapse after eculizumab discontinuation, whereas recurrence occurs in less than 10% of patients without identified pathogenic variants. \n\n### **Biochemical Assays for ComplementMediated aHUS** \n\nProteins constituting the complement system and its regulators are found both in fluid-phase and cell-bound and can be measured through standardized assays. However, in aHUS testing of complement activity is challenging and does not always reflect disease activity. \n\nSerum levels of complement factor 3 (C3) are commonly measured; decreased levels indicating consumption through hyperactivation of the complement cascade (Fig. 38.4). Complement regulating factors, FH and FI, can also be measured. Elevated plasma levels of the soluble C5b-9 complex (sC5b-9) suggest an activation of the terminal complement step in the fluid phase. The functional total complement assay (CH50) assesses the total residual complement activity in the sample. The result is given in percentage and reflects the dilution at which 50% hemolysis is seen. Results above 46% demonstrate normal activity, while a value below 10% indicates complete inhibition of the pathway and can be used to monitor complement inhibition therapy. The presence of anti-FH antibodies should also be tested in children with suspected C-aHUS. \n\nIn C-aHUS, complement activation occurs mostly at the endothelial level, while the complement cascade in the fluid phase is effectively controlled. As a result, the circulating complement profile is of limited significance and about a half of the patients display normal serum C3 levels and plasma sC5b-9 levels, even during an acute episode. \n\nTo better reflect disease pathogenesis, an _in vitro_ functional test has been developed to directly evaluate complement deposition on the endothelium. This assay quantifies the amount of serum-induced C5b-9 formation on human microvascular endothelial cells (HMEC-1) through confocal microscopy, reflecting the propension of the patient’s serum to generate complement activation products on the endothelium. Testing with unstimulated endothelium demonstrated deposits only with serum from patients with acute C-aHUS, thus allowing differentiation between an active phase and disease remission. Testing with adenosine diphosphate-activated endothelium demonstrates elevated C5b-9 deposits for all untreated patients with C-aHUS, independent of disease activity, documenting that C-aHUS patients have an intrinsic defect in complement regulation at cell surface level. A condition of clinical remission may be shifted to full blown disease in the presence of stimulus that alter such unstable equilibrium on endothelial cells. \n\n### **_DGKE_ Gene-Associated aHUS** \n\nBiallelic pathogenic variants in _DGKE_ have been associated with early-onset aHUS in children (Table 38.3). \n\n_DKGE_ encodes an intracellular enzyme (diacylglycerol kinase ε), that is expressed in endothelial cells, platelets and podocytes. This disease is not related to complement because _DGKE_ is not an integral protein of complement; patients do not usually have complement consumption. Loss-of-function of _DGKE_ in endothelial cells results in a prothrombotic state, likely related to upregulation of prothrombotic factors, decreased VEGF signaling and apoptosis. The clinical presentation is also remarkable for the presence of persistent hypertension, hematuria and proteinuria, which may be in the nephrotic range, as well as a high proportion of progression to chronic kidney disease. \n\n### **Cobalamin Metabolism-Associated aHUS** \n\nAtypical HUS can be associated with inborn errors of cobalamin (or vitamin B12) intracellular metabolism (Table 38.3). There are many subtypes of inherited disorders of cobalamin (cbl) metabolism (cbl A-J). CblC-type, also named methylmalonic aciduria and homocystinuria,-cblC type (MMA-cblC type), is the prototype of this family of diseases. MMA-cblC type has an autosomal recessive digenic inheritance, associated with pathogenic variants in _MMACHC_ and/or the neighboring _PRDX1_ , the latter resulting in _MMACHC_ silencing. The biallelic variants can occur both in the same gene (more common in _MMACHC_ ) or one in each. \n\nThe lack of the protein encoded by _MMACHC_ causes an intracellular deficiency of the metabolically active forms of cobalamin (methylcobalamin and adenosylcobalamin), which are essential cofactors for remethylating homocysteine to methionine and converting methylmalonic acid to succinic acid. Their functional deficiency thus results in high levels of homocysteine and methylmalonic acid (MMA) in plasma and urine, as well as low levels of methionine. Plasma levels of cobalamin and folate are normal and macrocytic anemia may not be present. \n\nTotal homocysteine levels may exceed 2–5 times the upper limit of normal; the level of serum vitamin B12, folate, urinary or plasma methylmalonic acid, plasma methionine and blood acylcarnitine profile are required to exclude or confirm any intracellular cobalamin disorders and to discriminate among the different forms. \n\nNinety percent of patients reported had an early onset of the disease. Among the remaining 10% of cases with a later onset, 30% are diagnosed during adulthood (>18 years). The late-onset form in adults is likely underdiagnosed. The early-onset form is characterized by lethargy, failure to thrive, developmental delay, neurological symptoms, microcephaly, ocular abnormalities and pulmonary arterial hypertension. The late-onset form of cblC-type disease is characterized by peripherical and central nervous system progressive symptoms, psychiatric symptoms, and venous and arterial thromboembolic complications. Ocular manifestations are rarer and pulmonary arterial hypertension has not been reported in the late onset form. \n\nAtypical HUS presents similarly both in the early-onset and late-onset forms of cblC-type deficiency. Occurrence is\n\n<!-- p.854 -->\n\nmore frequent in the late-onset forms (up to 30%) than the early-onset (10–20%), but mortality is extremely high in infants once aHUS has developed. \n\nKidney involvement may manifest as proteinuria, sometimes in the nephrotic range, or chronic kidney disease. Microangiopathic nephropathy may be present in the complete absence of the laboratory changes typical of TMA. When typical histological features of TMA are observed on a renal biopsy performed for idiopathic chronic kidney disease, the homocysteine assay should thus be included in the diagnostic evaluation. \n\nAtypical HUS has been observed in rarer subtypes such as cobalamin D- (cblD), cobalamin E- (cblE), and cobalamin G (cblG)-type disorders, respectively, associated to _MMADHC_ , _MTRR_ , and _MTR_ with an autosomal recessive inheritance. Clinical presentation overlaps with the cblC defect. Biochemically, non-clbC forms are characterized by macrocytic anemia, hyperhomocysteinemia and hypomethionemia, but with low or normal levels of MMA, as opposed to the cblC-form, where MMA is elevated. \n\nBecause the specific treatment with parenteral hydroxocobalamin is highly effective, the measurement of total homocysteine level is strongly recommended in any patient with aHUS, regardless of age, to not miss this treatable disease. Due to expanded newborn screening programs, early diagnosis and treatment of intracellular cobalamin metabolism disorders is possible in some countries. \n\n### **Denys–Drash Syndrome-Associated aHUS** \n\nDenys–Drash syndrome is a rare autosomal dominant disorder characterized by early-onset nephrotic syndrome with rapid progression to renal failure during the first few years of life, pseudohermaphroditism and a high risk of developing Wilms tumor. _WT1_ gene pathogenic variants are associated with the disease. The gene encodes a protein that regulates progenitor cells and their differentiation, especially those of the gonads, uteri, and kidneys. It is suggested that podocyte dysfunction results in decreased secretion of VEGF, which contributes to the development of renal TMA. \n\nThere are only anecdotal reports of Denys–Drash syndrome presenting with aHUS (Table 38.3). All progressed to end-stage renal disease despite treatment with plasma exchange, eculizumab, or both. No recurrences of aHUS occurred after kidney transplantation. \n\n### **Inverted Formin 2-Mediated Renal Disease** \n\nInverted Formin 2 _(INF2)_ gene encodes an actin regulating protein and is an autosomal dominant with incomplete penetrance form of **focal segmental glomerulosclerosis** , in association with **Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease** in 25% of the cases. \n\nPathogenic variants in _INF2_ in association with known aHUS risk haplotypes have been described in two families with either aHUS or posttransplant TMA, who did not respond to terminal complement-blockade with eculizumab (Table 38.3). TMA occurrence in INF2-related nephropathy \n\nis likely related to direct or indirect (via impaired podocyte secretion) endothelial injury or procoagulant state, similar to that reported in other causes of nephrotic syndrome. \n\n### **Primary Hyperoxaluria-Associated aHUS** \n\nA case of aHUS in association with primary hyperoxaluria type 2, due to biallelic _GRHPR_ pathogenic variants, has been described in a 6-month-old infant. These rare genetic autosomal recessive inborn errors of glyoxylate metabolism cause an overproduction of oxalate, resulting in kidney stones and systemic tissue deposition (Table 38.3). In this case, hyperoxaluria was hypothesized to have contributed to complement activation in the presence of a concomitant CFHR1-CFHR4 copy number variation, underlying the necessity of a combination of factors to cause endothelial lesions. \n\n### **Glucose-6-Phosphate Dehydrogenase (G6PD)Deficiency and Suspected aHUS** \n\nA child with clinical presentation suggestive of aHUS without any known secondary or genetic cause and nonresponsive to eculizumab was subsequently diagnosed with G6PD-deficiency (Table 38.3). This X-linked disorder of erythrocytes is associated with hemolysis in case of oxidative stress, caused by medication, acute illness or certain foods. Acute kidney injury can be seen in other cases of severe hemolysis, secondary to tubular lesions caused by excessive filtration of free hemoglobin and heme. Thrombocytopenia, however, is not typical of G6PD-deficiency. \n\n### **aHUS Associated With** **_TREX1_ Gene Pathogenic Variants** \n\nIn a Chinese family with kidney-predominant TMA, a C-terminal heterozygous frameshift variant in _TREX1_ has been identified (Table 38.3). TREX1 is a DNA-specific 30 to 50 exonuclease that has a vital role in DNA repair, protein glycosylation, and innate immune regulation. Pathogenic variants in _TREX1_ are associated with several disorders, including **retinal vasculopathy with cerebral leukoencephalopathy and systemic manifestations (RVCL-S)** . This autosomal dominant microvascular disease presents with predominant cerebroretinal vasculopathy. Impaired kidney function can also occur. Kidney predominant TMA is the key feature in different pedigrees with a specific frameshift variant at codon 247 in _TREX1_ (Asp247 _fs_ ), suggesting this is a hotspot that could contribute to predominantly kidney-related phenotype. The precise pathophysiological mechanisms are unclear at present, but do not seem to be related to complement dysregulation. \n\n## **Treatment** \n\nSupportive therapy remains fundamental in any form of HUS, in association with specific treatment of the underlying cause when identified.\n\n<!-- p.855 -->\n\nAnemia and thrombocytopenia may be severe and require transfusions, based on standard thresholds. Gastrointestinal losses may induce hypovolemia; oliguria can result in fluid overload, and fluid management should thus be tightly controlled. Hypertension requires medication in addition to appropriate fluid management. Medication doses must be adjusted to the glomerular filtration rate, with avoidance of nephrotoxic drugs. Electrolyte disturbances need regular monitoring. Kidney replacement therapy should be initiated according to standard indications. \n\nPlasma therapy, administrated as infusions or plasma exchanges has been the only available therapy. Both provide normal complement proteins; plasma exchanges also remove any potential circulation autoantibody or dysfunctional complement protein. Neither address the causative mechanism. Plasmapheresis was previously recommended within the first 24 hours of diagnosis of aHUS, although contrasting results in the literature with uncertain benefit mitigated this recommendation. It remains a therapeutic option when eculizumab is unavailable and, in the case of severe TMA with suspicion of TTP, awaiting diagnostic confirmation. \n\n### **Treatment of C-aHUS** \n\nBecause the activation of the terminal complement pathway is essential in the pathogenesis of C-aHUS causing complement-induced vascular lesions, terminal complement blockade with eculizumab (or ravulizumab) is approved for the treatment of C-aHUS and is associated with normalization of hematological parameters and improved renal function. Eculizumab should be started as soon as possible (Table 38.5). \n\nRavulizumab is a longer-acting complement C5 inhibitor, whose extended half-life allows for a less frequent \n\n\n#### **TABLE 38.5** Eculiz Uremi umab Dose for T c Syndrome reatment of Hemolytic\n\n|Body|||\n|---|---|---|\n|Weight (kg)|Induction|Maintenance|\n|>40|900 mg weekly<br>×4 doses|1200 mg at week 5;<br>then 1,200 mg<br>every 2 weeks|\n|30–40|600 mg weekly<br>×2 doses|900 mg at week 3;<br>than 900 mg every<br>2 weeks|\n|20–30|600 mg weekly<br>×2 doses|600 mg at week 3;<br>then 600 mg every<br>2 weeks|\n|10–20|600 mg weekly<br>×1 dose|300 mg at week 2;<br>then 300 mg every<br>2 weeks|\n|5–10|300 mg weekly<br>×1 dose|300 mg at week 2;<br>then 300 mg every<br>3 weeks|\n\n\n**TABLE Ravulizumab Dose for Treatment of 38.6 Hemolytic Uremic Syndrome** \n\n|Body<br>Weight (kg)|Induction|Maint|enance|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|10–20|600 mg|600 mg|Every 4<br>weeks|\n|20–30|900 mg|2,100 mg|Every 8<br>|\n|30–40|1,200 mg|2,700 mg|weeks|\n|40–60|2,400 mg|3,000 mg||\n|60–100|2,700 mg|3,300 mg||\n|>100|3,000 mg|3,600 mg||\n\n\nadministration while maintaining an equivalent efficacy and safety profile. \n\nThe dosage recommendation is also adapted according to weight (Table 38.6). \n\nMonitoring of disease activity is based on the serial assessment of markers of microangiopathic hemolysis: platelet count, serum levels of LDH, bilirubin and haptoglobin. Monitoring hemoglobinuria may be useful for early detection of disease reactivation, although interpretation is limited in women of childbearing age and can raise false concerns. \n\nThe optimal duration of treatment with eculizumab in patients with C-aHUS is uncertain. Evidence supports that eculizumab can be successfully discontinued, particularly in the absence of known complement genetic abnormalities and when there is no history of previous relapses. \n\nIn patients with pathogenic variants in complement genes, discontinuation of eculizumab is hazardous, in particular in those with _CFH_ abnormalities, whereas absence of complement gene variants has a lower risk of relapse. \n\nIn patients with and without genetic abnormalities, treatment discontinuation must only be considered in the absence of any sign of disease activity and after a minimum of 3–6 months of treatment. In patients with advanced chronic kidney disease following C-aHUS, the risk of reaching ESKD in case of recurrence is particularly high. Patients with ESKD undergoing kidney replacement therapy rarely relapse after discontinuing eculizumab, and it is reasonable to stop the treatment once kidney failure is deemed irreversible, bearing in mind the potential multiorgan involvement of TMA beyond renal manifestations, notably gastrointestinal, neurological and cardiovascular. \n\nA useful parameter for monitoring therapy tapering is the quantification of serum-induced C5b-9 formation on cultured endothelial cells. Atypical HUS serum-induced C5b-9 formation on activated and unstimulated endothelium normalized during eculizumab treatment. During eculizumab dosage tapering or after treatment discontinuation, all patients experiencing relapses versus only 6% of those in\n\n<!-- p.856 -->\n\nstable remission had elevated C5b-9 deposits on unstimulated endothelium. \n\nAnother proposed monitoring scheme using CH50 is to increase the interval between doses by one week if CH50 before the planned administration is totally suppressed. CH50 is then redetermined downstream and should remain below 30%, or else the interval should be shortened again accordingly. \n\nRegardless of the chosen treatment plan, the interval between doses should be reduced during acute events (infections, illnesses, trauma, surgery, vaccinations), because of the expected increased synthesis of acute phase proteins, including C5. \n\nPatients receiving anti-C5 therapy are at risk for infection with encapsulated organisms and should receive antimicrobial prophylaxis as well as appropriate vaccination against _Neisseria meningitis_ , _S. pneumoniae_ and _Haemophilus influenza type b_ . \n\nIn patients with proven anti-FH antibodies, immunosuppression with corticosteroids, with or without cyclophosphamide, rituximab or mycophenolate mofetil is recommended following plasma exchange. \n\nDespite the progress in therapy, a significant number of patients with C-aHUS will develop ESKD. Before C5blockade was available, the recurrence posttransplant was as high as 60%. Currently with eculizumab treatment reducing the risk of disease relapse, an increasing proportion of patients benefit from successful transplantation. Complement genotyping before kidney transplantation is mandatory to evaluate the risk of recurrence. When a related living donation is being considered, potential donors should also be tested to exclude the same pathogenic variants because this would impose an unacceptable risk for the donor of developing C-aHUS and kidney disease in the future. \n\nCombined liver-renal transplantation has been performed in severe cases and is the only curative approach because most complement proteins are synthetized by the liver (FH, FI, FB, C3). Patients must undergo a preparation with complement regulation (plasma therapy, complement blockade) prior to the intervention. This complex intervention carries a 15% mortality risk and a careful evaluation of the relative risks and benefits is mandatory. Nevertheless, it may be the only treatment option for C-aHUS cases in countries where long-term anti-C5 therapy is not available. \n\n## **Treatment in** **_DGKE_ Gene-Associated aHUS** \n\n_DGKE_ encodes an intracellular enzyme and is unrelated to complement, making eculizumab ineffective in most cases. Treatment thus remains in line with the general management of aHUS and relies on symptomatic treatment of TMA and nephrotic syndrome when present, as well as nephroprotection. This includes adequate blood pressure control, transfusion, renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system blockade and metabolic control. Plasmapheresis is of uncertain benefit. Kidney transplant is usually successful with a low risk of recurrence. \n\n## **Treatment in Cobalamin MetabolismAssociated aHUS** \n\nSpecific treatment of these inborn errors of metabolism aims to normalize serum methionine and lower both homocysteine and MMA as quickly as possible. The cornerstone of treatment in cblC-, cblE-, and cblG-type disorders is parenteral hydroxocobalamin; _cyanocobalamin is ineffective_ . It must be administrated without waiting for identification of the specific defect in any suspected inborn errors of cobalamin metabolism because it significantly improves survival and reduces the incidence of severe complications. Hydroxocobalamin therapeutic dosage is high (0.3 mg/kg/ day) and must be titrated to obtain very high serum cobalamin levels (near 1,000,000 pg/mL, compared to a normal range of 170–800). One report of a small group of patients with early-onset disease suggested a much higher dose of hydroxocobalamin (5–7 mg/kg/day) and demonstrated an improved long-term outcome. \n\nTreatment includes a combination of folinic acid and, in cases of free carnitine deficiency, levocarnitine (at a dosage of 50–200 mg/kg/day). To lower homocysteine levels, it may be necessary to administer oral betaine in 3 to 4 doses/ day, given its short half-life. The initial dose is 250 mg/kg/ day (maximum dose 20 g/day). Still, the minimum effective dose must then be found, as betaine causes an increase in methionine, which at very high levels (>1,000 μmol/L) can cause acute cerebral edema. \n"});
